{"audio":{"src":"https://pub-13abeb101e2945d2a7f60234f6faf52e.r2.dev/materials/three-famous-food-markets-in-europe/audio/main-e0a80fe6a812.mp3","durationSec":168.144,"cues":[{"start":0,"end":4.8,"text":"La Boqueria stands on La Rambla, but its story began before the covered market."},{"start":5.52,"end":8.76,"text":"In 1217, records described meat stalls there."},{"start":9.56,"end":14.42,"text":"Many early sellers were farmers from villages around Barcelona, bringing food into the city."},{"start":15.44,"end":18.32,"text":"Traders moved to the present site in 1836."},{"start":19.08,"end":21.82,"text":"Today, more than 200 traders open their shutters."},{"start":21.82,"end":28,"text":"Visitors pass bright fruit and vegetables, olives and preserved foods, meat, fish and seafood."},{"start":28.74,"end":30.88,"text":"Small bars serve food beside the shopping stalls."},{"start":31.66,"end":37.18,"text":"The market still connects two groups: people who sell food and people who come to shop, eat and talk."},{"start":37.7,"end":41.72,"text":"It has a long history, but it is also part of everyday city life."},{"start":41.72,"end":46.52,"text":"Marché des Enfants Rouges is Paris's oldest covered food market."},{"start":47.18,"end":50.28,"text":"Its unusual name means “Market of the Red Children.”"},{"start":51.4,"end":54.32,"text":"It remembers children from a nearby home who wore red clothes."},{"start":54.76,"end":58.84,"text":"In the 1990s, there was a plan to replace the old market with a car park."},{"start":59.72,"end":63.02,"text":"Traders and neighbours protested and even slept outside to save it."},{"start":63.9,"end":66.82,"text":"Today, their market is full of different people and foods."},{"start":67.68,"end":70.5,"text":"Tom's team serves Moroccan chicken skewers and couscous."},{"start":71.38,"end":74.02,"text":"Julienne cooks Creole food with flavours from Cameroon."},{"start":74.98,"end":82.18,"text":"Visitors can also buy farm cheese, Breton buckwheat galettes, oysters, fresh fruit juice, and organic fruit and vegetables."},{"start":82.18,"end":89.8,"text":"Borough Market's story goes back about 1,000 years, when people sold food near London Bridge."},{"start":90.74,"end":95.6,"text":"In 1756, local parishioners moved it to the place where it stands today."},{"start":96.36,"end":98,"text":"The market is now a charity."},{"start":98,"end":107.2,"text":"Volunteer trustees run it for shoppers, traders and neighbours, so it is still a community place, not only a tourist sight."},{"start":107.2,"end":109.48,"text":"More than 100 businesses sell food there."},{"start":109.88,"end":117.78,"text":"Applebee's offers British seafood, Arabica serves Middle Eastern meze, falafel and shawarma, and Bao Borough makes bao buns."},{"start":118.74,"end":125.12,"text":"Visitors can shop for cheese and other ingredients, choose a hot lunch, and meet some of the people who feed London today."},{"start":126.52,"end":129.6,"text":"Tipping customs change between Spain, France and the UK."},{"start":130.34,"end":137.16,"text":"At a simple market stall or self-service counter, people normally pay the displayed price unless the seller says otherwise."},{"start":138.1,"end":142.04,"text":"In Spain, a tip is optional, and some people simply round up the bill."},{"start":142.84,"end":147.96,"text":"In France, restaurant prices normally include service, so extra money is optional."},{"start":148.86,"end":154.36,"text":"In the UK, a restaurant bill may include a discretionary or compulsory service charge."},{"start":155,"end":157.92,"text":"A visitor should read the bill before adding more money."},{"start":159.1,"end":162.54,"text":"The rules can feel different, but you do not need to guess."},{"start":162.98,"end":167.7,"text":"The safest habit in all three countries is simple: check the bill, then ask."}],"words":[{"text":"La","start":0,"end":0.22},{"text":"Boqueria","start":0.22,"end":0.66},{"text":"stands","start":0.66,"end":1.08},{"text":"on","